WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.679
Film Courage: I almost picture it like the
kid in the class that finishes the test before

00:00:03.679 --> 00:00:07.780
everyone else and everyone looks around like
how did they finish that so fast?

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:12.589
If we take that same analogy with a new writer
who kind of says “I just want to get my

00:00:12.589 --> 00:00:13.589
work out there.

00:00:13.589 --> 00:00:15.259
I don’t really need all of these drafts.

00:00:15.259 --> 00:00:16.390
I know this is good.”

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:19.660
Wendy Kram, owner of LA4Hire: You know, it’s
possible.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:24.539
Someone might be a savant of screenwriting
and able to do it.

00:00:24.539 --> 00:00:33.500
Every great screenwriter that I’ve seen,
the ones who get all of the awards, they always

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.809
say that it’s taken them 30, 40 drafts.

00:00:37.809 --> 00:00:45.719
There’s a top, top, one of the most powerful
show runners in television history and she

00:00:45.719 --> 00:00:49.960
said (and this might be an obvious thing to
say) but she says “A lot of people say they

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:55.780
want my life, but they are not able to put
in the hours and the blood, sweat and tears”

00:00:55.780 --> 00:01:02.120
I have another friend who is a top female
show runner and I know the hours that she

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.769
works and it’s not a 40-hour-a-week.

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:08.390
Sometimes it’s an 80-hour-week.

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:16.610
She loves her job and on weekends sometimes
she’s there until Midnight on a Friday and

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:21.840
Saturday working eight to twelve hours and
then the weekdays.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:35.799
So like Mozart, I think he wrote his amazing
compositions and he was somewhat of a savant.

00:01:35.799 --> 00:01:46.119
So I think it possible but something that
gets in the way…I understand the enthusiasm

00:01:46.119 --> 00:01:52.750
and excitement to want to get your script
out there but there can be either naivety

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:56.470
or hubris.

00:01:56.470 --> 00:02:00.930
And I’ve noticed that there are a lot times
when the writer (and it could be the same

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:07.200
with an actor) that they are more focused
on the end results and I know for me (as the

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:11.020
producer and the executive), I’m focused
on the end result.

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:15.440
There is nothing I want more than a movie
to get made.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:19.670
It is the greatest high after you’ve gone
through all this work and seeing it come to

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:25.770
fruition and seeing the set designer create
the sets and your actors actually performing

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.140
the lines in the scenes.

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:36.050
But if you look at every wonderful writer,
director, filmmaker, actor, the trajectory

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:43.460
of their careers, they started out like doing
little bit parts and movies that you never

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:48.280
would have thought they would have ended up
one day getting an Oscar.

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:56.800
So each step is a part of the process of the
journey.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:03.400
I used to say whether it was someone like
Kathryn Bigelow when she started out or Patty

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:09.470
Jenkins (for example) when Patty did MONSTER
and I think she’d gone to AFI, she just

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:11.010
kept doing good work.

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:12.880
These are people who just kept doing good
work.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:18.940
I don’t think that Patty Jenkins thought
when she was in AFI “Oh, I’m going to

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:20.611
do an indie film called MONSTER.”

00:03:20.611 --> 00:03:29.400
And get an Oscar for the leading actress and
then I’m going to do WONDER WOMAN, superhero

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:35.620
and be the first woman to break all box office
records as the director.

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:41.450
Barry Jenkins when he did MOONLIGHT, I don’t
think he was doing it because he thought “Oh,

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:42.610
I’m going to get an Oscar.”

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:46.840
I think he was compelled to want to tell a
story.

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:52.220
And he told it beautifully and it affected
and resonated with so many people.

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:59.910
So I think the most important thing for writers,
directors, actors is to stay committed to

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:05.540
the craft and just doing the best work that
you can and surround yourself with people

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:11.350
who give you really constructive feedback
even if it’s not always what you want to

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:12.440
hear.

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:17.709
But to be open to feedback that can be constructive
and make you a better writer.

00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:20.320
Film Courage: Do you think that’s a factor
of age?

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:25.350
Do you think young people really want to rush
through it or it’s not about that, it can

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:26.350
vary.

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:28.070
Wendy Kram: In my experience, it’s not about
that.

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:37.460
Because I know older writers who feel like
time is not on their side so they actually

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:41.230
in some ways can be in more of a rush than
younger writers.

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:46.730
Film Courage: When you see a writer that you
know that they do have talent and you can

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:50.970
see that it maybe just needs a little more
refining and you need to slow them down, what

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:52.810
are some of the things that you do?

00:04:52.810 --> 00:05:00.930
Wendy Kram: First of all, I don’t like to
ever feel that I’m in the position to slow

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:01.930
someone down.

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:07.470
Most of what I do is just ask questions.

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:14.870
In the script there are plot aspects or character
behavior that is not clear to me or confusing

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:21.590
or inconsistent, I’ll just ask the question
and I’ll always tell them never to feel

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:23.990
compelled to agree with me.

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:25.570
It’s not a dictatorial thing.

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:33.050
But it is for the purpose of I feel I’m
a pretty good barometer of how other executives

00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:34.740
are going to respond to material.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:39.140
And if it’s not clear to me then there’s
a good chance that it might not be clear to

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:40.280
someone else.

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:46.060
So they never feel like I want to impose something
to be done a certain way but I will raise

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:51.590
a question or I’ll say “I feel this dialogue,
it doesn’t feel authentic to me.

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:55.110
It feels like there’s an agenda, the writer
has an agenda.”

00:05:55.110 --> 00:06:00.650
That you want the character to say this but
I think if we’re really looking in the scene

00:06:00.650 --> 00:06:05.180
and the daughter is responding to her mother,
how would she really respond?

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:12.830
And I’ll ask the writer to go back and kind
of try to find the truth rather than the written

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:16.510
agenda (if that makes sense)?

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:21.090
So I think it’s really more about asking
questions and pointing out areas that can

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:24.460
be improved rather than slowed down.

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:28.840
Like if you think it’s ready to go and you’re
happy with it and you don’t agree than by

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:32.050
all means you should pursue that.

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:39.050
But I think a lot times there can be a tendency
to take something out there too hastily.

00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:44.061
Film Courage: Do you think people are aware
of how damaging that can be because if they

00:06:44.061 --> 00:06:51.180
get consistent coverage it just kind of labels
that one project or even their name (as not

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:52.180
ready)?

00:06:52.180 --> 00:07:02.680
Wendy Kram: Because here’s the thing, a
lot of writers will be focused on networking

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:05.150
which is great, networking is important.

00:07:05.150 --> 00:07:09.520
Let’s say you meet someone as a producer
and they say “Great!

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:11.070
Send me your script.”

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:18.690
And it’s not very good, you have a frozen
moment in time to capture that person’s

00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:22.700
attention and if they don’t respond to it
or they have an assistant who reads it as

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:27.120
says (because a lot of times that is what
happens) and says “It wasn’t very good.”

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:33.060
The next time you want to get something in,
they might not be as inclined to read your

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:34.060
work.

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:36.910
“Tell them it’s a pass.

00:07:36.910 --> 00:07:41.120
The writing was just not up to snuff, it was
very amateurish.

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:44.530
The person doesn’t even know how to write
a script to be honest.”

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:50.470
There are certain things where there is classic
formatting but there is also certain telltale

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:58.150
signs such as if there is dialogue on the
page that takes up half the page or almost

00:07:58.150 --> 00:08:03.169
the whole page, that’s the telltale sign
of (with exception) of course there are always

00:08:03.169 --> 00:08:08.590
exceptions but that would be a sign that it’s
an amateur.

00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:15.280
If there are pages in a script that are so
dense that it looks like a novel, that would

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:16.280
be another sign.

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:23.900
If there’s like page after page of very
dense descriptions, that will wind up in the

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:29.259
recycle bin or the trash in your computer.

